\label{def:DiagonalOperator}

An operator ${\mathfrak D}$ acting on $
    \def#2{}
    \ifx#2\empty
        \Gamma L^2_{{\mathrm{rap}}}
    \else
        \Gamma H^{}_{{\mathrm{rap}}}
    \fi
$ is said to be \emph{diagonal} if there exists a family of symmetric kernels $d = (d_n)_{n \geq 0}$ such that, for any $\varphi \in 
    \def#2{}
    \ifx#2\empty
        \Gamma L^2_{{\mathrm{rap}}}
    \else
        \Gamma H^{}_{{\mathrm{rap}}}
    \fi
$, it holds
${\mathfrak F}({\mathfrak D} \varphi) (k_{1:n}) = d_n(k_{1:n}) \, {\mathfrak F} \varphi(k_{1:n})$ for every $k_{1:n} \in \mathbb{R}^{2n}$, $n \geq 0$ (to avoid pathologies, we always assume that the kernels are measurable and grow at most as $|d_n(k_{1:n})| \lesssim n^a \, (1 + |k_{1:n}|^b)$ for some $a, b \geq 0$). Given two diagonal operators ${\mathfrak D}^{(1)}$ and ${\mathfrak D}^{(2)}$, we write ${\mathfrak D}^{(1)} \leq {\mathfrak D}^{(2)}$ if their respective kernels $d^{(1)}$ and $d^{(2)}$ satisfy $d_n^{(1)}(k_{1:n}) \leq d_n^{(2)}(k_{1:n})$ for every $k_{1:n} \in \mathbb{R}^{2n}$, $n \geq 0$.
Finally, given a diagonal operator ${\mathfrak D}$ with kernel $d = (d_n)_{n \geq 0}$
and a scalar function $f$, we define (when it makes sense) $f({\mathfrak D})$ as the diagonal operator
whose kernel is $f(d) = (f \circ d_n)_{n \geq 0}$.

We define the diagonal operators ${\mathfrak N}$, ${\mathfrak N}_*$, ${\mathfrak L}_0$, ${\mathfrak L}_0^\mathfrak{e}$, ${\mathfrak L}_0^\mathfrak{f}$ whose respective kernels on chaos $n$ are given by: $n$, $1 \vee n$, $- |k_{1:n}|^2/2$, $- |\mathfrak{e} \cdot k_{1:n}|^2/2$, $- |\mathfrak{f} \cdot k_{1:n}|^2/2$.

\label{lem:Llim}
On $
