{\noindent \it Step 3. The combinatorial analysis}

We first introduce some combinatorial notation and facts, that can be found in~\cite[Section 2.5]{peccati2011wiener}. Let $\Pi_n$ be the set of partitions of $\{1, \dots, n\}$, equipped with the refinement order: that is $\pi \leq \pi'$ if any block of $\pi$ is included in a block of $\pi'$. If $\pi \in \Pi_n$ and $1 \leq k \leq n$, then we denote by $\pi_k$ the set of blocks of size $k$ of $\pi$. Let $\hat{0} \in \Pi_n$ be the singleton partition $\{\{1\}, \dots, \{n\}\}$. Let $\mathrm{Pair}_n \subset \Pi_n$ be the set of partial pairings, that is partitions whose blocks are of size $1$ or $2$. The Möbius function corresponding to the poset $(\Pi_n, \leq)$ is  given by
\begin{equ}
\mu(\pi) \stackrel{\mbox{\tiny\rm def}}{=} \prod_{B \in \pi} (-1)^{|B|-1} \, (|B|-1)! \, .
\end{equ}
Note that, if $\pi \in \mathrm{Pair}_n$, then $\mu(\pi) = (-1)^{\# \pi_2}$. Since $\hat{0}$ is the minimum element of $\Pi_n$, the Möbius inversion formula gives that, for any $\pi \in \Pi_n$,
\begin{equ} \label{e:MobiusInv}
\1_{\pi = \hat{0}} = \sum_{\pi' \in \Pi_n} \mu(\pi') \, \1_{\pi' \leq \pi} \, .  
\end{equ}
Finally, if $x_{1:n} \in \mathbb{R}^{2n}$, then we denote by $p(x_{1:n}) = \hat{0}$ the corresponding ``equality'' partition, that is $i$ and $j$ belong to the same block if and only if $x_i = x_j$. Observe that $p(x_{1:n}) = \hat{0}$ if and only if $x_{1:n}$ satisfies the hard core condition; note also that for any given $\pi \in \Pi_n$, it holds $p(x_{1:n}) \geq \pi$ if and only if $(x_i)_{1 \leq i \leq n}$ is constant on each block of $\pi$.
\medskip


Now, we come back to~\eqref{e:CVchaosGoal2} and rewrite the two appearing summands. On the one hand, by recalling $Z_t^\tau(h) = \sum_{x \in \mathbb{Z}^2} h_t^\tau(x) \, \sigma(x)$ and the formula~\eqref{e:Hermite} for the Hermite polynomial (recall also that $c_{k,n}$ is the number of partial pairings with $k$ blocks of size $2$ and $n-2k$ blocks of size $1$), we have
\begin{equs}
H_n(Z_t^\tau(h), \|h\|_{L^2(\mathbb{R}^2)}) &= \sum_{0 \leq k \leq n/2} c_{k,n} \, (-1)^{k} \, \|h\|_{L^2(\mathbb{R}^2)}^{2 k} \, \Big(\sum_{x \in \mathbb{Z}^2} h_t^\tau(x) \, \sigma(x)\Big)^{n - 2k} \\
&= \sum_{\pi \in \Pi_n} \1_{\pi \in \mathrm{Pair}_n} \, \mu(\pi) \, \|h\|_{L^2(\mathbb{R}^2)}^{2 \# \pi_2} \, \Big(\sum_{x \in \mathbb{Z}^2} h_t^\tau(x) \, \sigma(x)\Big)^{\# \pi_1} \, . \label{e:HermiteZ}
\end{equs}
On the other hand, by using that $x_{1:n} \in HC$ if and only if $p(x_{1:n}) = \hat{0}$, we have
\begin{equ}
I(\mathchoice
              {\setbox1\hbox{${\displaystyle (h_t^\tau)^{\otimes n}}_{\scriptstyle \mathbb{Z}^{2n} \cap HC}$}
              {(h_t^\tau)^{\otimes n}\,\smash{\vrule height .8\ht1 depth .85\dp1}}_{\,\mathbb{Z}^{2n} \cap HC}}
